{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"f2a47d6b-373e-4e26-b16a-7fa50ccd3034","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No windows/ directory — product code not delivered. So tests must output \"待测：模块未交付\" and skip. But we're also asked to write xUnit tests that reference ErrorPresenter/LogRedactor/InviteCode.IsValid with namespace TBD at top. The challenge: if the product namespace doesn't exist, the test project won't compile. The task says \"产品代码未交付时：测试必须清楚输出“待测：模块未交付”并以 pytest skip（或脚本退出码 0 + 该提示）结束，不能伪装通过。\" For .NET/xUnit, compiling against a missing type fails. So we need a design where the test project can compile without product code and skip at runtime, clearly stating \"待测：模块未交付\".\n\nOptions:\n1. Write xUnit tests referencing types via reflection (no compile-time dependency), so the test project compiles regardless. At runtime, if type not found, emit \"待测：模块未交付\" and use Assert.Skip (xUnit v2.5+ / v3 supports Assert.Skip). But dependency — xUnit itself is a dependency. The task explicitly says write xUnit tests, so xUnit package reference is required and authorized by the task card. We're not adding new dependencies beyond what asked.\n\nHowever, the namespace is \"待对齐放顶部\" — put namespace as constant at top. Reflection approach avoids compile errors and lets us skip when types missing. But the task says \"被测类型名 ErrorPresenter/LogRedactor/InviteCode.IsValid 来自 brief，命名空间待对齐放顶部\". This suggests define namespace constants at top for later alignment. Reflection seems reasonable and robust: we don't know namespace. But could also create a test csproj that ProjectReferences windows project — doesn't exist, so build fails. Can't do that.\n\nBut the task likely expects xUnit source files with `using OsakaConnect...` and constants. Yet when product isn't delivered, the test project cannot build. To satisfy \"产品代码未交付时输出待测：模块未交付 and exit 0\", reflection + skip is the minimal robust approach. But is reflection \"testing user-visible behavior\"? It still calls the methods and asserts outputs. Reflection is a bit ugly but necessary to compile without product code and to keep namespace TBD.\n\nAlternative: use conditional compilation with a symbol, e.g. `#if PRODUCT_AVAILABLE`. Too much.\n\nAlternative: Write tests using dynamic? `dynamic` requires Microsoft.CSharp, and namespace still needs to resolve at runtime — `Activator.CreateInstance` with string type names. Reflection is cleaner.\n\nNeed a csproj, xunit packages, and test files. Only allowed to modify files under tests/windows/win-diagnostics/. Create:\n- tests/windows/win-diagnostics/WinDiagnostics.Tests.csproj\n- tests/windows/win-diagnostics/TestPaths.cs (repo root resolution / vector loading)\n- tests/windows/win-diagnostics/ErrorPresenterTests.cs\n- tests/windows/win-diagnostics/LogRedactorTests.cs\n- tests/windows/win-diagnostics/InviteCodeTests.cs\n- maybe README.md with run commands and \"待 CI\" marker\n- maybe .github workflow? Not allowed — only files under tests/windows/win-diagnostics/. The task says \"脚本面向 GitHub Actions，本机不装 JDK/SDK/.NET、不跑构建；脚本顶部标注“待 CI”.\" So just note it. Can we edit tests/INDEX.md? Task says only tests/windows/win-diagnostics/. So no.\n\nNeed to decide xunit version. xUnit v3 supports Assert.Skip; xUnit v2.5+ also has Assert.Skip? Let me recall: xUnit v2.5 (released 2024) added `Assert.Skip`? Actually, `Assert.Skip` was introduced in xUnit v3. In v2, dynamic skip can be done via `throw new SkipException(...)` from Xunit.SkippableFact package (extra dependency). To avoid extra dependency, xUnit v3 with Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk? Hmm, xUnit v3 uses `xunit.v3` package and `xunit.runner.visualstudio`. xUnit v3 supports dynamic skip via `Assert.Skip(\"reason\")`, and also `Assert.SkipWhen(condition, reason)`. Alternatively `[Fact(Skip=\"...\")]` is static skip — but we need to conditionally skip based on runtime detection. Actually since product code isn't in this worktree and might be delivered later, dynamic detection at runtime is right.\n\nBut xUnit v3 needs .NET 8 SDK. Windows-latest has .NET 8. Packages restore from NuGet in CI. Fine.\n\nYet pinning package versions that I can't verify offline is risky. Common versions: xunit.v3 1.0.0 released Dec 2024. Today in repo context is 2026-11-02, so xunit.v3 likely 3.x. Unknown. Since we cannot run restore offline, choose known stable versions? Could use xunit 2.9.2 + xunit.runner.visualstudio 2.8.2 + Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 17.11.1. For dynamic skip in v2, not available without SkippableFact. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: use xUnit v2 and simply return early (test passes) after writing \"待测：模块未交付\" to console. But that \"can't fake pass\" — a passing test is a hidden pass. The task says skip OR exit code 0 with prompt. For xUnit, static skip is possible if we could decide at compile time. We could implement custom FactAttribute that checks at discovery/execution? Too much.\n\nBetter: xUnit v3 with `Assert.Skip`. Let me check what versions are plausible. As of late 2025, xunit.v3 3.0.x exists (3.0.0 released May 2025?), xunit.v3 3.1.0? I recall xunit.v3 1.0.0 Dec 2024, 2.0.0 Feb 2025? Actually xUnit.net v3 1.0.0 released Dec 2024; then 2.x maybe. Hmm. Pin a floating version? Use `Version=\"3.*\"`? Floating versions in CI restore work (`3.0.*`). But offline can't restore anyway. Since tests run in CI, floating minor is acceptable but less reproducible. Could use `xunit.v3` with Version=\"3.0.1\"? I'm not certain.\n\nMaybe safer: target xUnit v2 and use `[Fact]` with a convention: if product missing, `Assert.Skip` unavailable. Hmm, in xUnit v2.9 there is `Assert.Skip`? Let me recall changelog. xUnit v2.5.0 (2023-11) added... no. Dynamic skipping was a v3 feature. In v2, test authors used `Xunit.SkippableFact` package. Adding that is an extra dependency, which violates \"不新增依赖\" unless justified. The test framework itself is necessary, but extra skip package is avoidable by v3.\n\nAnother approach: Since the test project is new and independent, use a plain .NET console \"test runner\" instead of xUnit? No — task explicitly says xUnit.\n\nWe could write xUnit tests with a custom `[Fact]` variant? Static skip needs discovery-time info. Discovery runs in the test process; a custom FactAttribute could set Skip at construction? Attributes are constructed at reflection time before running; `Skip` property could be set in constructor based on product presence, but attribute constructor runs during discovery in test host where type detection via reflection works. That's clever: define `ProductFactAttribute : FactAttribute` that in constructor checks whether the product type can be loaded; if not, sets `Skip = \"待测：模块未交付\"`. This works with xUnit v2! Minimal custom attribute. But it needs to evaluate product availability at discovery; assembly loading may behave fine. However, the product assembly doesn't exist at all in this repo (no windows/). Later when delivered, tests need a ProjectReference added — but we're not allowed to modify outside tests dir, and we can't know project path. Hmm. Could reference via assembly path? Better: test csproj can conditionally ProjectReference if file exists? MSBuild conditions can check existence at evaluation time:\n\n```xml\n<ItemGroup Condition=\"Exists('$(RepoRoot)windows/.../WinDiagnostics.csproj')\">\n  <ProjectReference Include=\"...\" />\n</ItemGroup>\n```\n\nBut we don't know the csproj name/path. The task says \"被测类型名 ... 来自 brief，命名空间待对齐放顶部\" and \"需要产品入口时，在脚本顶部集中定义成常量/待定项，并注释'待写手交付后对齐'\". So placeholders for assembly name/namespace are expected at top. Reflection with an assembly name constant and namespace constant at top, plus a mechanism to locate the product assembly? When a ProjectReference is added, types are in the test app's load context and `Type.GetType(\"Namespace.Type, Assembly\")` works if assembly name known. Alternatively scan loaded assemblies + AppDomain. But if no ProjectReference, the assembly isn't loaded. We can