{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"64817764-3e7c-4649-8e64-5fd1239e467f","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me think harder about whether the skip mechanism actually produces \"待测：模块未交付\" visibly. In xUnit v2, the skip reason is reported. dotnet test console output summarizes \"Skipped! ... [reason]\" with `--logger \"console;verbosity=detailed\"`, but default output shows \"Skipped: 3, Passed: 0, Failed: 0\" and may not show the reason unless detailed. The requirement says \"测试必须清楚输出'待测：模块未交付'\". Skip reason may be buried. To guarantee clear output, we could also print to stdout in a module initializer or assembly fixture when product missing.\n\nOption: Add an `AssemblyFixture`? xUnit v2 doesn't have assembly fixtures built-in. Could use a class fixture or a test that always runs and prints? An always-running test that prints \"待测：模块未交付\" and passes would be a fake pass if counted. But if it's a separate test that asserts skip? Hmm.\n\nWe can add a module initializer (`[ModuleInitializer]`) that writes to Console.WriteLine when the product type is missing. But module initializers run when the test assembly loads, and output goes to the test host console; dotnet test captures? Console output from tests is shown on failure or with detailed logger, not guaranteed. Still, it writes clearly.\n\nAlternatively, the skip reason itself appears in TRX output. The task says output the message; skip reason qualifies. The Python precedent uses pytest.skip reason shown in output. For xUnit, skip reasons are part of the result (and full output under detailed verbosity). I think it's acceptable; README documents it and suggests verbosity.\n\nCould we make skip explicit with `dotnet test --logger \"console;verbosity=detailed\"`? Update README run command to include? Keep normal command; tests will report skipped. Add note: use `--logger \"console;verbosity=detailed\"` to see the skip reason. Good.\n\nMaybe better: define a custom test framework? No.\n\nAdd README note:\n```\n默认 `dotnet test` 摘要会显示 Skipped；查看跳过原因加 `--logger \"console;verbosity=detailed\"`。\n```\nLet me edit.\n\nAlso, consider adding to `ProductSurface` a module initializer that writes the message when product missing:\n\n```csharp\ninternal static class DeliveryNotice\n{\n    [ModuleInitializer]\n    internal static void AnnounceIfMissing()\n    {\n        if (ProductSurface.TryResolve(ProductSurface.ErrorPresenterType) is null\n            && ProductSurface.TryResolve(ProductSurface.LogRedactorType) is null\n            && ProductSurface.TryResolve(ProductSurface.InviteCodeType) is null)\n        {\n            Console.WriteLine(ProductSurface.NotDelivered);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\nNeeds `using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;`. This guarantees a clear line in the test host output when the assembly loads, though capture varies. Adds little. Is it worth it? The requirement is \"必须清楚输出\". Skip reason is clear. But module initializer output may be swallowed by dotnet test unless detailed. Hmm.\n\nActually, xUnit v2's `dotnet test` default console logger does show `[xUnit.net ...] ... Skipped <test name>`? Let me recall: `dotnet test` output typically ends with \"Passed! - Failed: 0, Passed: 0, Skipped: 3, Total: 3\" without reasons. With `--logger \"console;verbosity=normal\"` it shows per-test only failed. So the Chinese reason might not show! That could fail the requirement's spirit.\n\nTo force the message, we could write it to a file? No.\n\nWe could avoid skip-at-discovery and instead use a test that runs, writes the message, and then... exit code 0 without passing? xUnit counts it passed if no exception. That's \"伪装通过\" per the rules. Not acceptable. Could throw a special exception? Then failed, not exit 0.\n\nCould use xUnit's `Assert.Skip` (v3) which still is a skip with reason — same visibility.\n\nCould run `dotnet test` with detailed logger in CI; we can document, but the actual CI wiring is ci-guards'.\n\nAdd a module initializer writing to Console.Out and Console.Error. At least the message is emitted by the process. When `dotnet test` runs, test host stdout is usually captured and shown for failed tests; for skipped maybe not. But with `--logger console;verbosity=detailed` it will show. Also TRX includes the skip reason.\n\nAlternatively, create an MSBuild target `BeforeTargets=VSTest` that checks for windows/ and prints, but the check needs to be runtime (product could exist). An MSBuild target:\n\n```xml\n<Target Name=\"WarnIfProductMissing\" BeforeTargets=\"VSTest\">\n  <Message Importance=\"high\" Condition=\"!Exists('$(ProductProject)')\" Text=\"待测：模块未交付（win-diagnostics）\" />\n</Target>\n```\nThis prints to the `dotnet test` output always, independent of test output. Nice. But message target before VSTest may run in the test project build. `BeforeTargets=\"VSTest\"` works with Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk. There's also `VSTest` target. We can use `BeforeTargets=\"VSTest\"` with Condition. This is a clear output mechanism and simple. Add to csproj:\n\n```xml\n<Target Name=\"AnnounceProductMissing\" BeforeTargets=\"VSTest\">\n  <Message Importance=\"high\" Condition=\"!Exists('$(ProductProject)')\"\n           Text=\"待测：模块未交付（win-diagnostics；未找到 $(ProductProject)）\" />\n</Target>\n```\nIf the product project exists but types/method names don't align, the skip reason appears in results and detailed logs. That's fine.\n\nThis satisfies \"清楚输出\". Add it.\n\nWait: if `ProductProject` exists, no message; tests run. Good.\n\nAlso if product assembly delivered at a different path and `-p:ProductProject=` points to it, Exists true.\n\nLet me add this target to csproj.\n\nAlso note: `dotnet test` without building? It builds. VSTest target runs after build. Message appears.\n\nLet me edit csproj.","signature":"64817764-3e7c-4649-8e64-5fd1239e467f"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":508,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":54272,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec7e012c-bf97-4a00-b1a5-b32ee1d3c38b","uuid":"f771a1ca-57cb-4b57-8e55-479a87aee860","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:04:55.370Z","thinking_duration_ms":8858,"thinking_display":"updates"}
