{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"d3284b82-a993-4884-ac5a-fa02f9ab3af4","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"One potential compile issue in ErrorPresenterTests: `Assert.Equal(entry.Zh, shown);` where shown is `string?`. In xunit 2.9, `Assert.Equal<T>(T expected, T actual)` with T inferred as string? works. Fine.\n\nBut `Assert.DoesNotContain(\"at \", shown!)` — after `Assert.False(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(shown), ...)`, shown is not null but compiler doesn't know; `shown!` silences.\n\nLogRedactor same.\n\nOne concern: xUnit analyzer xUnit2012? no.\n\nNow think about `FullyQualifiedName` filter in README: class has no namespace so FQN is `ErrorPresenterTests.A3_...`; `~ErrorPresenterTests` matches. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: `[ModuleInitializer]` not used.\n\nNow maybe add a test to verify that `errors.json` is fully covered? The loop covers all.\n\nOne thing I want to double-check: the xUnit attribute class name must end with \"Attribute\"; `[ProductFact(...)]` resolves to `ProductFactAttribute`. Good.\n\nPotential issue: `ProductFactAttribute` constructor calls `TryResolve`, which calls `Assembly.Load` for each test method discovery (4 methods). Each call attempts load and throws FileNotFoundException; minor. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: `Assembly.Load` by simple name when assembly not referenced will throw `FileNotFoundException`, caught. Good.\n\nPotential issue: In single-file/AOT? No.\n\nNow a bigger concern: When the product project IS delivered and referenced, will `Assembly.Load(new AssemblyName(\"OsakaConnect.Windows\"))` load it before any type is used? The test assembly's deps.json includes it; Assembly.Load by name resolves from the app's trusted platform / deps. In .NET Core, `Assembly.Load(AssemblyName)` uses the default AssemblyLoadContext, which resolves from deps.json. Yes.\n\nNow, a thought: the product might be a WPF app with `OutputType=WinExe`; referencing an exe is allowed. Yes.\n\nNow, let's think about whether `ProductSurface.Call` handles a presenter method that returns `string` but is an instance method on a class with no parameterless ctor. That would fail; documented alignment. Fine.\n\nNow, could the writer implement ErrorPresenter as a static class? GetMethods finds static. Fine.\n\nNow, the task says \"被测类型名 ErrorPresenter/LogRedactor/InviteCode.IsValid 来自 brief\". We use those. Good.\n\nNow check ponytail requirement: \"new non-trivial logic leaves one small test or an assert-based self-check\". Our new test harness itself is tested by the tests. No extra needed.\n\nCould I add a self-check test that the vectors file loads and has expected counts? The actual tests do `Assert.NotEmpty` and iterate; if JSON mapping fails, tests fail. Good.\n\nOne subtle JSON issue: `invite-code.json` invalid list contains `null`. `System.Text.Json` deserializing `null` into `List<string?>` yields null element. Good.\n\nNow, potential issue: JSON `redaction.json` has \"in\"/\"out\" keys; we map with JsonPropertyName. Good.\n\nPotential issue: `errors.json` keys map to ErrorEntry; Http and Zh via case-insensitive options. Good.\n\nNow, the `Message` task output: `BeforeTargets=\"VSTest\"` — does the target exist? In Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk, the `VSTest` target is defined (in Microsoft.TestPlatform.targets). Yes. If it runs before `VSTest`, message prints. Even if not, it's a warning-free no-op. Could use `BeforeTargets=\"VSTest;Test\"` to be safe. `Test` target exists. Add both? `BeforeTargets=\"VSTest;Test\"` might print twice. Keep VSTest.\n\nNow, final check of git status for files. Already listed.\n\nShould I also create a result log path? No run.\n\nThe final answer: list 7 files + one sentence. Must not add extra lines? \"完成后只回复：新建的文件清单 + 一句话说明。\" So format:\n\n新建文件：\n- tests/windows/win-diagnostics/WinDiagnostics.Tests.csproj\n- ...\n一句话：...\n\nInclude note \"待 CI，本机未运行\" in the sentence. Fine.\n\nWait — before final, let me reconsider a potentially fatal incompatibility: xunit 2.9.2's `FactAttribute` and custom attribute. There's a known xUnit analyzer error xUnit1000? Let me recall custom attributes like `[SkippableFact]` derive from FactAttribute — standard. The xUnit test discovery code in v2 finds methods with an attribute whose type is assignable to FactAttribute. Actually, I recall xUnit v2 discovers by looking for methods with attributes named \"FactAttribute\" or deriving? The `XunitTestFrameworkDiscoverer` uses `ITestFrameworkDiscoverer` and `Xunit.Sdk.FactDiscoverer`? It scans for attribute type assignable to `FactAttribute`. Yes, derived works — this is how SkippableFact works.\n\nBut a subtle point: xUnit's analyzer requires custom Fact attributes to be named `*FactAttribute` and have a parameterless ctor? Our ctor has a string parameter; xUnit instantiates via the attribute's own constructor (reflection), which requires the argument supplied in source. That's fine—the instance is created with the string we passed. No parameterless needed. Discovery code gets the attribute instance from reflection. Yes.\n\nOne more: `Skip` may be `init`-only in newer xunit versions, but we pin 2.9.2 where it's settable. Let me be more certain: xunit 2.9.2 FactAttribute source:\n\n```csharp\npublic class FactAttribute : Attribute\n{\n    public virtual string? DisplayName { get; set; }\n    public virtual string? Skip { get; set; }\n    public virtual bool Explicit { get; set; }\n    public virtual int Timeout { get; set; }\n}\n```\n\nYes, I'm fairly confident.\n\nPotential issue: xUnit v2 analyzers (xunit.analyzers 1.16) may throw xUnit1000 on `ProductFactAttribute` because it sees a public method? no methods.\n\nNow, about C# `is { } found` with `GetType(...)` returning `Type?` — property pattern `{ }` matches non-null. Requires C# 8+. LangVersion latest. Fine.\n\n`for (var dir = new DirectoryInfo(...); dir is not null; dir = dir.Parent)` — `dir` inferred `DirectoryInfo` (non-nullable) but assigned `dir.Parent` which is `DirectoryInfo?` → nullable warning CS8600? Actually `var dir` type is `DirectoryInfo`, assigning `DirectoryInfo?` to it gives CS8600 warning \"Converting null literal or possible null value to non-nullable type\". Warning, not error. But then condition `dir is not null` is redundant/always true from compiler's view? Not an error. To avoid warnings, declare `DirectoryInfo? dir`. Let me fix:\n\n```csharp\nfor (DirectoryInfo? dir = new(AppContext.BaseDirectory); dir is not null; dir = dir.Parent)\n```\nThis is cleaner. Does `DirectoryInfo?` with target-typed new work in for initializer? Yes C# 9. But `new(AppContext.BaseDirectory)` target-typed. Fine. Or `new DirectoryInfo(...)`. Let me edit to avoid warnings.\n\nSimilarly, `method.Invoke(target, args)` where target may be null for static — fine.\n\n`Activator.CreateInstance(type)` returns object?; assigned to `var target` with conditional `method.IsStatic ? null : Activator...` → type object?; fine.\n\nIn ProductSurface, `AppDomain.CurrentDomain.GetAssemblies()` implicit using System. Fine.\n\nEdit SharedVectors for nullable.","signature":"d3284b82-a993-4884-ac5a-fa02f9ab3af4"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1873,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":58112,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ec7e012c-bf97-4a00-b1a5-b32ee1d3c38b","uuid":"1ef6d3e9-a3ce-4dd4-81f8-80a559e369af","timestamp":"2026-11-02T05:05:18.347Z","thinking_duration_ms":10956,"thinking_display":"updates"}
